1. Title 

Design and Development of a Speaker-Aware ASR Pipeline for Body-Worn Camera Audio 

2. Abstract 

Body-worn camera audio is a challenging and under-researched domain for automatic speech recognition because it combines conversational speech with outdoor noise, movement, changing microphone distance, interruptions, overlapping speakers, and short context-dependent utterances. This research investigates the design requirements for a practical speaker-aware ASR system in this domain, where existing research and ready-made solutions remain limited. The study examines the full audio processing path, including audio extraction, preprocessing, voice activity detection, speaker diarization, automatic speech recognition, transcript formatting, and evaluation against ground truth. The technical approach considers how ASR systems operate in controlled conditions, how conversational ASR differs from single-speaker transcription, and how recognition quality changes in uncontrolled outdoor environments. The research draws on pyannote diarization, NVIDIA Riva ASR, NVIDIA Parakeet, Sortformer diarization, MarbleNet VAD, DeepFilterNet enhancement, and custom comparison tooling to develop an audio pipeline suited to body-worn camera recordings. The findings indicate that body-worn camera audio should be treated as a specialized conversational ASR problem rather than a standard transcription task. Clean conversational recordings produce stronger results, while body-worn camera style recordings expose persistent challenges in speaker attribution, missed short utterances, noise handling, and diarization boundaries. This paper presents the research motivation, system design, technical decisions, and remaining challenges for reliable speaker-aware transcription of body-worn camera audio. 

