4. Introduction 

Automatic Speech Recognition, or ASR, is widely used to convert spoken language into written text. Many ASR systems perform well on clean recordings, dictation, interviews, and studio-style conversational audio. Body-worn camera recordings present a more difficult setting because they capture speech in uncontrolled environments where the microphone moves with the officer, speaker distance changes continuously, and important utterances may be short, interrupted, shouted, or mixed with background noise. 

This research addresses the problem of speaker-aware transcription for body-worn camera audio. The research considers ASR from multiple levels: the general operation of speech recognition systems, the added complexity of conversational speech, and the degradation that occurs when conversation takes place outdoors or in noisy public environments. This framing is necessary because body-worn camera recordings combine conversational dynamics with difficult acoustic conditions. 

The focus of the research is converting noisy body-worn camera recordings into text while preserving who spoke and when. This distinction is important because a transcript that recognizes words but assigns them to the wrong speaker may be misleading in operational or compliance review. The problem is therefore broader than simple word recognition and requires attention to segmentation, speaker attribution, voice activity detection, diarization, and audio quality. 

Body-worn camera audio can support review of compliance, escalation, safety, visit outcomes, and operational decision-making. However, the domain is difficult because recordings may contain wind, traffic noise, sirens, clothing rustle, microphone friction, radio chatter, overlapping speakers, far-field civilian speech, and rapid speaker changes. The officer is usually close to the microphone, while other speakers may be distant or masked by the environment. These conditions can cause missed speech, false speech detections, poor speaker boundaries, and officer/non-officer speaker confusion. 

The purpose of this research is to identify and develop an audio processing pipeline appropriate for body-worn camera recordings. Rather than treating the task as a simple selection of an off-the-shelf ASR model, the research examines the design choices required for this domain, including preprocessing, VAD, diarization, ASR model selection, transcript structure, and evaluation methodology. The paper presents the rationale behind these choices and describes the limitations that remain because body-worn camera audio is still a relatively under-explored ASR use case. 

5. Research Gap and Motivation 

Automatic Speech Recognition has advanced significantly in recent years, particularly for clean speech, dictation, meeting transcription, call-center recordings, and general conversational audio. Many modern ASR systems can produce accurate transcripts when the recording conditions are controlled, the microphone is stable, and the speech signal is clear. However, body-worn camera audio represents a more complex and less explored environment. It combines conversational speech with outdoor noise, movement, unstable microphone placement, overlapping speakers, short utterances, and rapid changes in speaker distance. 

Existing ASR work often focuses on word-level transcription accuracy, but body-worn camera audio requires more than a plain transcript. In operational review, it is important to know not only what was said, but also who said it, when it was said, and whether any speech was missed or incorrectly assigned. A transcription system that produces words without reliable speaker attribution can be insufficient or misleading, especially in scenarios involving compliance, escalation, safety, and post-incident review. 

This creates a research gap between general-purpose ASR systems and the requirements of body-worn camera audio analysis. Body-worn camera recordings require a pipeline that combines multiple audio intelligence components, including voice activity detection, preprocessing, speaker diarization, ASR, transcript structuring, and error-aware evaluation. Each component affects the final transcript quality. For example, poor VAD can remove short but meaningful replies, diarization errors can merge officer and civilian speech, and ASR errors can distort the actual content of an interaction. 

The motivation for this project is to move beyond basic transcription toward speaker-aware operational conversational intelligence for body-worn camera audio. Instead of treating ASR as a standalone task, this work investigates how different audio processing stages can be combined into a structured pipeline suitable for noisy, real-world enforcement recordings. This is especially important because existing research on body-worn-camera-specific ASR and diarization remains limited compared with research on cleaner conversational speech. 

